ChatGPT said:
Absolutely. If a user reports “DNS is not resolving”, a technical-support engineer should troubleshoot it systematically—from confirming the symptom to identifying whether the problem is with the client, DNS server, network, or domain itself.

DNS troubleshooting: Start to end
1. First, understand the problem
Ask the user:

What website/application are you trying to access?
Is the issue happening on one device or multiple devices?
Can you access websites by IP address?
When did the issue start?
Are you connected through Wi-Fi, LAN, VPN, or a corporate network?
Is the problem affecting all websites or only a specific domain?
Do other users have the same problem?
For example:

User can access 8.8.8.8 but cannot access google.com.
That strongly suggests a DNS-resolution problem rather than a general internet-connectivity problem.

2. Check basic network connectivity
Before troubleshooting DNS, make sure the machine actually has network connectivity.

Windows
Open Command Prompt:

ipconfig /all
Check:

IPv4 address
Default Gateway
DNS Servers
DHCP enabled
Whether the IP is something like 169.254.x.x
A 169.254.x.x address usually indicates that the computer did not receive an IP address from DHCP.

Then test the gateway:

ping <default-gateway>
Example:

ping 192.168.1.1
If the gateway cannot be reached, don't start changing DNS settings yet. You likely have a network/connectivity problem.

3. Test internet connectivity without DNS
Try:

ping 8.8.8.8
You can also test:

ping 1.1.1.1
Interpret the result
If ping succeeds:

Reply from 8.8.8.8...
Internet connectivity is probably working, so investigate DNS.

If ping fails:

Don't immediately conclude that the internet is down—ICMP may be blocked. Test with another method, such as:

curl https://1.1.1.1
or open a known website/application.

4. Check the configured DNS server
Run:

ipconfig /all
Look for:

DNS Servers . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.1.1
or a corporate DNS server such as:

10.10.10.10
10.10.10.11
Ask:

Is this the DNS server that this machine is supposed to use?

For a corporate environment, don't randomly replace the corporate DNS server with Google or Cloudflare DNS. Internal domains may only resolve through the organization's DNS infrastructure.

5. Use nslookup — one of your most important DNS tools
Run:

nslookup google.com
You might see:

Server:  dns.company.local
Address: 10.10.10.10

Non-authoritative answer:
Name:    google.com
Addresses: 142.250.x.x
This tells you that DNS resolution is working.

If you see something like:

DNS request timed out.
timeout was 2 seconds.
or:

*** dns.company.local can't find example.com: Server failed
then you have evidence of a DNS-resolution problem.

6. Test a specific DNS server
This is extremely useful for isolating the problem.

Try:

nslookup google.com 8.8.8.8
Then:

nslookup google.com 1.1.1.1
Now compare the results.

Example
Your company's DNS:

nslookup google.com 10.10.10.10
fails.

But:

nslookup google.com 8.8.8.8
works.

This suggests that the problem may be with the corporate DNS server or the path to it, rather than the user's computer's basic network connectivity.

However, before changing the user's DNS, investigate why the corporate DNS is failing.

7. Check whether only one domain is affected
Suppose the user says:

“I can't access app.company.com.”
Run:

nslookup app.company.com
Then test another domain:

nslookup google.com
Scenario A — Everything fails
google.com       → fails
microsoft.com    → fails
company.com      → fails
Likely causes:

DNS server unavailable
Incorrect DNS configuration
Network/VPN problem
DNS service problem
Firewall blocking DNS
DHCP assigning incorrect DNS servers
Scenario B — Only one domain fails
google.com       → works
microsoft.com    → works
app.company.com  → fails
Now investigate:

DNS record missing
Incorrect DNS record
Internal DNS zone problem
Split-DNS/VPN configuration
Domain-specific DNS server issue
8. Clear the local DNS cache
Windows maintains a local DNS cache.

Run:

ipconfig /flushdns
You should see:

Successfully flushed the DNS Resolver Cache.
Then test again:

nslookup google.com
and try the application/website again.

9. Renew the IP configuration
If the DNS information comes from DHCP, renew the lease:

ipconfig /release
ipconfig /renew
Then:

ipconfig /all
Check whether the correct:

IP address
Default gateway
DNS server
have been assigned.

10. Test DNS connectivity directly
DNS normally uses UDP port 53, and sometimes TCP port 53.

You can test TCP connectivity to a DNS server using PowerShell:

Test-NetConnection 10.10.10.10 -Port 53
Example:

ComputerName     : 10.10.10.10
RemotePort       : 53
TcpTestSucceeded : True
If TCP 53 fails, it doesn't necessarily prove DNS is broken because normal DNS queries commonly use UDP, but it is useful evidence when investigating firewall/network issues.

11. Check the DNS service/server
If you are supporting the DNS infrastructure, go to the DNS server.

For Windows Server environments, use:

DNS Manager

You can open:

Server Manager
   ↓
Tools
   ↓
DNS
Check:

DNS server is running
Forward Lookup Zones
DNS records
Forwarders
Conditional forwarders
Root hints
Event Viewer
Server network connectivity
You can also check the DNS service from PowerShell:

Get-Service DNS
You want to see something similar to:

Status   Name   DisplayName
------   ----   -----------
Running  DNS    DNS Server
12. Check the DNS record
Suppose the problem is:

server01.company.com
Run:

nslookup server01.company.com
Check whether it returns the expected IP.

For example:

Name:    server01.company.com
Address: 10.20.30.40
If there is no answer, check the DNS zone.

In DNS Manager:

Forward Lookup Zones
       ↓
company.com
       ↓
server01
Verify that the A record exists and points to the correct IP.

For IPv6, also check the relevant AAAA record.

13. Use Resolve-DnsName
PowerShell provides a more detailed DNS troubleshooting tool:

Resolve-DnsName google.com
You can also specify a DNS server:

Resolve-DnsName google.com -Server 8.8.8.8
For an internal domain:

Resolve-DnsName server01.company.com -Server 10.10.10.10
This is often more useful than simply using ping.

14. Check DNS suffix configuration
For corporate environments, this can be very important.

Run:

ipconfig /all
Look for:

Primary DNS Suffix
Connection-specific DNS Suffix
DNS Suffix Search List
For example:

DNS Suffix Search List:
company.com
If a user types:

ping server01
the system may attempt:

server01.company.com
Incorrect DNS suffix configuration can therefore cause internal name-resolution problems.

15. If VPN is involved, investigate Split DNS
This is a very common corporate-support scenario.

Example:

The user connects to VPN and tries:

internal-app.company.com
It doesn't resolve.

But:

google.com
works.

Possible cause:

VPN didn't push corporate DNS servers
DNS suffix wasn't pushed
Split-DNS configuration is incorrect
VPN client configuration is broken
Corporate DNS isn't reachable through the VPN
Check:

ipconfig /all
and:

nslookup internal-app.company.com
Then compare DNS configuration before and after connecting to VPN.

16. Check firewall/security software
If the DNS server is correct but queries aren't reaching it, investigate:

Windows Firewall
Corporate firewall
Endpoint security
Network firewall
VPN security policies
DNS filtering/security products
You can use packet capture when necessary.

Tool: Wireshark
Wireshark official website

Capture traffic while running:

nslookup google.com
Look for DNS traffic:

Client → DNS Server : UDP 53
DNS Server → Client : UDP 53
You can filter Wireshark with:

dns
or:

udp.port == 53
This lets you determine whether:

The DNS request leaves the client.
The request reaches the DNS server.
The DNS server responds.
The response makes it back to the client.
17. Check DNS server forwarding
Suppose:

Client
   ↓
Corporate DNS
   ↓
Internet DNS
The client may successfully reach the corporate DNS, but the corporate DNS might be unable to resolve external domains.

On the DNS server, check Forwarders.

For Windows DNS:

DNS Manager
   ↓
DNS Server
   ↓
Properties
   ↓
Forwarders
Check whether the configured forwarders are:

reachable
responding
correctly configured
18. Check authoritative DNS
For public-domain troubleshooting, use:

nslookup
or PowerShell:

Resolve-DnsName example.com
You can also use external DNS diagnostic services when appropriate.

A useful concept is to determine whether the problem is:

Client DNS
     ↓
Recursive DNS
     ↓
Root DNS
     ↓
TLD DNS
     ↓
Authoritative DNS
For advanced troubleshooting, you can use:

dig example.com
On Linux/macOS, for example:

dig example.com
and:

dig @8.8.8.8 example.com
19. Compare with another working computer
This is one of the easiest ways to isolate the problem.

Take:

Problem PC

DNS Server: 10.10.10.10
nslookup internal-app.company.com → FAIL
Working PC

DNS Server: 10.10.10.10
nslookup internal-app.company.com → SUCCESS
Now you know the DNS server itself is probably working, and the investigation should focus on the affected machine/network path.

Conversely, if many computers fail, investigate the DNS infrastructure.

20. Determine the root cause
At this point, categorize the issue.

Finding	Likely cause
No IP address	DHCP/network problem
Gateway unreachable	Network connectivity problem
Internet IP works but names don't	DNS problem
Wrong DNS server configured	DHCP/network configuration
DNS server unreachable	Network/firewall/VPN
DNS server reachable but doesn't respond	DNS server/service issue
Only one domain fails	DNS record/zone issue
Internal domains fail only on VPN	VPN/split-DNS issue
DNS works after clearing cache	Local DNS cache problem
Multiple users affected	Infrastructure/DNS-server issue
21. Apply the appropriate fix
Don't use the same fix for every DNS problem.

If client DNS configuration is wrong
Correct the DNS configuration through the organization's approved method—usually DHCP, Group Policy, MDM, or network configuration.

If DNS cache is corrupted
ipconfig /flushdns
If DHCP provided incorrect DNS
Fix the DHCP scope/options rather than manually changing every workstation.

If DNS server service has failed
Restart/repair the DNS service according to your organization's change-management procedure.

If a DNS record is incorrect
Correct the relevant:

A record
AAAA record
CNAME record
PTR record
MX record, depending on the problem
If VPN DNS is broken
Correct the VPN/DNS configuration so corporate DNS is available when the VPN is connected.

22. Verify the fix
Never stop immediately after making the change.

Run:

ipconfig /flushdns
Then:

nslookup affected-domain.com
Then:

Resolve-DnsName affected-domain.com
If appropriate, test:

ping affected-domain.com
Remember: successful ping isn't required for DNS to work because ICMP may be blocked.

Finally, test the actual application or website the user was having trouble with.

For example:

DNS resolution: PASS
Network connectivity: PASS
Application access: PASS
User confirmed: PASS
23. Document the incident
A good technical-support ticket should contain:

Issue

User unable to resolve app.company.com.
Symptoms

User can access the internet, but app.company.com does not resolve.
Troubleshooting

Verified IP connectivity and default gateway. Confirmed incorrect DNS server configuration. nslookup failed against the configured DNS server. Compared configuration with a working workstation.
Root Cause

DHCP scope was providing an incorrect DNS server address.
Resolution

Corrected the DHCP DNS configuration, renewed the client's DHCP lease, flushed the DNS cache, and verified successful DNS resolution.
Validation

nslookup app.company.com successfully returned the expected IP address. User confirmed the application is accessible.
