If a user tells you “My computer is running slowly,” as a Technical Support Engineer you should troubleshoot it systematically rather than immediately deleting files or reinstalling Windows.

1. First, understand the complaint
Ask the user:

When did the computer start becoming slow?
Is it slow all the time or only when running a particular application?
Does it take a long time to boot?
Is the computer slow when opening applications?
Is the mouse/keyboard responding slowly?
Is the internet slow, or is the computer itself slow?
Does the issue occur after connecting to VPN?
How many applications/tabs are open?
Was any software recently installed or updated?
Has the computer recently received a Windows update?
Is the problem affecting only this user or multiple users?
Important distinction
You need to determine whether the problem is:

Computer performance

Applications take a long time to open, CPU is high, system freezes, etc.
or

Network performance

Websites/applications are slow, but the computer itself responds normally.
These are different troubleshooting paths.

2. Check Task Manager first
On Windows, press:

Ctrl + Shift + Esc
This opens Task Manager.

Go to:

Processes → Details/Performance

Check:

CPU
Memory
Disk
Network
GPU
Example
You might see:

CPU       95%
Memory    82%
Disk      100%
Network   5%
Now you have something measurable rather than simply “the computer is slow.”

3. Check CPU usage
Go to:

Task Manager → Processes → CPU

Sort by CPU usage.

For example:

Application             CPU
--------------------------------
Antimalware Service     45%
Chrome                  25%
Teams                   15%
Other                   5%
Identify which process is consuming the CPU.

Don't immediately terminate the process
First determine what it is.

For example:

Browser → investigate tabs/extensions
Teams → application issue
Antivirus → scan/update activity
Windows Update → update activity
Unknown executable → investigate further
If it is a legitimate application and you're authorized to close it, you can close it and check whether performance improves.

4. Check memory/RAM
In Task Manager:

Performance → Memory

Look at:

In use
Available
Committed
Cached
For example:

Memory: 15.5 GB / 16 GB
If memory is consistently close to maximum, the machine may be under memory pressure.

Then go to:

Processes → Memory

Identify which applications are consuming the most RAM.

Example:

Chrome       5.2 GB
Teams        1.5 GB
Outlook      1.0 GB
Other        5.0 GB
Close unnecessary applications/tabs if appropriate.

5. Check disk usage
This is one of the most important checks.

In Task Manager:

Performance → Disk

If you see:

Disk: 100%
go to:

Processes → Disk

Find which process is generating the disk activity.

Possible causes include:

Windows Update
Antivirus scan
OneDrive synchronization
Search indexing
Application activity
Low free disk space
Disk health problems
Excessive paging
6. Check available disk space
Open:

File Explorer → This PC

Check the system drive, usually:

C:
If the drive is nearly full, Windows performance can suffer.

You can also use:

Get-Volume
or:

wmic logicaldisk get size,freespace,caption
On newer Windows installations, PowerShell is preferable to relying on deprecated WMIC commands.

What should you do?
If storage is low:

Remove unnecessary temporary files
Empty Recycle Bin if appropriate
Remove unnecessary downloads
Use Windows Storage settings
Review large files
Remove approved/unneeded applications
Do not randomly delete files from C:\Windows or other system directories.

7. Check startup applications
A computer that takes several minutes to become usable after login may have too many startup applications.

Open:

Task Manager → Startup apps

Review applications such as:

Teams
OneDrive
Adobe applications
Updaters
Vendor utilities
Other unnecessary startup programs
Disable only applications that are safe to disable and permitted by your organization's IT policy.

Don't disable:

Security software
Required corporate agents
Management tools
VPN/security components
Unknown system components
8. Check running services
Open:

services.msc
You can investigate services consuming resources or related to the suspected application.

PowerShell can also help:

Get-Service
Don't start/stop random services. In a corporate environment, verify what the service does before changing it.

9. Check for Windows updates
Go to:

Settings → Windows Update

Check whether:

Updates are downloading
Updates are installing
A restart is pending
The machine recently completed a major update
Windows Update can temporarily increase:

CPU usage
Disk usage
Network usage
If an update is actively running, let it complete where appropriate and then reboot according to your organization's policy.

10. Check for malware/security activity
If the system is unexpectedly slow, investigate security software activity.

For Microsoft Defender, you can check Windows Security:

Windows Security → Virus & threat protection

Look for:

Active scan
Recent detections
Security alerts
Protection status
Do not disable antivirus simply because it is consuming CPU.

If malware is suspected, follow your organization's security/incident-response procedure.

11. Check Event Viewer
Open:

eventvwr.msc
Check:

Windows Logs
   ├── Application
   ├── System
   └── Security
For performance problems, pay particular attention to System and Application logs.

Look for recurring:

Disk errors
Application crashes
Service failures
Driver errors
Hardware-related errors
Don't treat every warning/error as the root cause. Look for events that correlate with the time the user experienced the problem.

12. Check disk health
If the computer has become progressively slower, freezes frequently, or has disk-related errors, investigate storage health.

You can check basic drive status with:

Get-PhysicalDisk
Look at:

HealthStatus
OperationalStatus
For example:

HealthStatus: Healthy
OperationalStatus: OK
If the drive reports problems, escalate according to your organization's hardware-support procedure.

For an SSD/HDD suspected of failing, don't repeatedly run aggressive repair operations before considering whether important data needs to be protected.

13. Check system files
If Windows itself appears unstable or corrupted, you can use Microsoft's built-in repair tools.

Open Command Prompt as Administrator and run:

sfc /scannow
This checks Windows system files.

If necessary, you can then use:

DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth
After completion, restart the computer and test again.

In an enterprise environment, follow your organization's standard procedure before running repair commands.

14. Check drivers
Open:

devmgmt.msc
This opens Device Manager.

Look for:

Yellow warning symbols
Display driver problems
Storage-controller issues
Network adapter problems
Recently changed hardware/drivers
Don't update every driver just because a newer version exists. Identify whether a driver is actually related to the symptoms.

15. Check whether the problem is application-specific
Suppose the user says:

“Everything is fine except Outlook is extremely slow.”
Don't troubleshoot the entire computer as if the hardware were failing.

Test:

Notepad → fast
File Explorer → fast
Chrome → fast
Outlook → slow
That suggests an application-specific problem.

Then investigate the specific application:

Application version
Add-ins
Cache
Profile
Local data
Network connectivity
Server/service status
Application logs
16. Check network performance separately
If the user says:

“Everything is slow when I use the internet.”
Check whether the computer itself is slow.

Test:

ping <default-gateway>
Then test an external destination where appropriate.

You can also use:

Test-NetConnection <hostname>
If only network applications are slow while Windows itself responds normally, investigate the network, not CPU/RAM.

17. Check VPN
Corporate users frequently experience perceived slowness after connecting to VPN.

Compare:

VPN disconnected → normal
VPN connected → slow
If this pattern exists, investigate:

VPN latency
VPN client
DNS
Routing
Bandwidth
Corporate proxy
Security inspection
Split tunneling configuration
This is especially important if applications hosted inside the company are slow but local applications work normally.

18. Check Resource Monitor
If Task Manager doesn't give enough information, use:

resmon.exe
Resource Monitor gives you more detailed information about:

CPU
Memory
Disk
Network
For example, under Disk, you can identify which process is performing significant disk I/O and which files are being accessed.

This is an excellent next step when Task Manager says Disk = 100% but you need more detail.

19. Use Performance Monitor for deeper investigation
For recurring or difficult performance problems, use:

perfmon.exe
Performance Monitor can track counters such as:

Processor utilization
Memory
Disk activity
Network activity
Process performance
This is more appropriate for advanced troubleshooting or problems that occur intermittently.

20. Check Reliability Monitor
Another very useful Windows tool is:

perfmon /rel
This opens Reliability Monitor.

It provides a timeline of:

Application crashes
Windows failures
Driver failures
Updates
Hardware-related problems
If the computer became slow starting on a particular date, Reliability Monitor can help you correlate the issue with an update, driver installation, or application failure.

